<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Painful swelling of the left lower jaw for four days after three weeks of toothache under a broken lower left molar, in a 48-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes found on screening today. Please examine and image the tooth, drain the abscess and give definitive treatment, assess her periodontal health, and advise on antibiotics and follow-up. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Urgent, within 24 hours. The swelling is localized to the buccal side of the lower left molars, with a temperature of 37.6 °C, no trismus, a soft floor of mouth and no neck swelling; she swallows and breathes normally. No antibiotic has been started, because drainage is arranged for tomorrow. She has been told to go to the emergency department at once for swelling spreading to the eye, the floor of the mouth or the neck, difficulty swallowing or breathing, trouble opening her mouth, or a fever. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is the source of the swelling, and what definitive treatment do you advise for the molar?</item><item>Does she need an antibiotic, given her new diabetes?</item><item>What is her periodontal status, and what periodontal treatment does she need?</item><item>Does her diabetes change her dental care or healing, and how does periodontal care relate to her diabetes?</item><item>What follow-up and maintenance do you advise?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Hatshepsut, a 48-year-old woman who directs a large public authority, is seen at the request of Dr. Okafor-Harrington the day after her first visit. A lower left molar broke about a month ago; it began to throb three weeks ago, and four days ago her gum and cheek beside it swelled. She has had no fever above 37.6 °C, no trouble opening her mouth, swallowing or breathing. She has not seen a dentist for several years and lost two back teeth earlier to gum disease. Type 2 diabetes was found yesterday, and she took her first metformin tablet last night. She has no known allergy. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Hatshepsut was a daughter of the king Thutmose I and became the chief queen of Thutmose II. [D]</item><item>After the death of Thutmose II she acted as guardian and regent for the young Thutmose III, her nephew and stepson; by his seventh regnal year she had taken the public role of a king, while still referred to with feminine forms. [D]</item><item>She claimed to have been born of a union between her mother and the god Amun-Ra, and looked to her father Thutmose I as the source of her kingship. [D]</item><item>Her birth year is not recorded. An age of about 50 at death, suggested by study of the mummy identified as hers, would place her birth about 1507 BC; this is a rough estimate. [R]</item></list><paragraph>8 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

