<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Acute periapical abscess of tooth 19 (K04.7). A localized acute apical abscess from a non-restorable fractured molar, without spread to the fascial spaces and without systemic involvement. Incision and drainage and extraction were performed today under local anesthesia, and pus drained freely. This is the definitive treatment. [S]</item><item>Periodontitis, generalized, stage III, grade C (K05.323). Interdental bone loss to the middle third of the roots with probing depths of 5 to 7 mm and two teeth lost to periodontitis place it at stage III; an A1c of 7.0% or more makes it grade C. Periodontitis and diabetes affect each other: periodontal inflammation worsens glucose control, and periodontal treatment lowers A1c by a few tenths of a percent. [S]</item><item>Type 2 diabetes and dental care (E11.65). Poorly controlled diabetes raises the risk of spreading infection and slows healing, so she needs close watch for spread over the next few days; with definitive treatment done and no systemic signs, an antibiotic is still not indicated. Better glucose control will help her gums heal. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Okafor-Harrington&apos;s intake note and results: A1c 7.6%, random glucose 212 mg/dL, white cell count 11.6, normal kidney function. Panoramic radiograph and a periapical film today: deep caries under a fractured crown of tooth 19 with a periapical radiolucency at both roots; generalized horizontal bone loss to the middle third of the roots, worst at the molars; teeth 3 and 30 missing; no other periapical lesion. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>No antibiotic: the abscess is localized, there is no systemic involvement, and drainage and extraction have been done; amoxicillin for five to seven days only if fever, spreading swelling or trismus develops. [S]</item><item>Ibuprofen with acetaminophen for a few days; no opioid. [S]</item><item>Chlorhexidine 0.12% rinse twice daily for two weeks, starting this evening; soft food on the right; sutures dissolve. [S]</item><item>Emergency department at once for swelling spreading to the eye, floor of mouth or neck, difficulty swallowing or breathing, trouble opening the mouth, or fever. [S]</item><item>Scaling and root planing of all teeth with the dental team once the socket has healed, in about six to eight weeks, then periodontal maintenance every three months. [S]</item><item>A replacement for the missing molars can be discussed once the periodontitis is controlled. [S]</item><item>Glucose control as Dr. Okafor-Harrington plans; the dental team will share the periodontal results. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Okafor-Harrington the same morning; she continues the diabetes care and will see Ms. Hatshepsut this afternoon. Ms. Hatshepsut was shown her radiograph and asked to keep her public engagements light for two days. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Review of the socket at one week by phone or in the dental clinic; scaling and root planing in about eight weeks; periodontal maintenance every three months. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
